WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:12.500
Hi everyone it's Jerry. This is a game from the
2004 Reykjavik Rapid between Magnus Carlsen

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:19.560
and Garry Kasparov. This was their very first
encounter. Carlsen still 13 years old and

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:26.560
an International master at the time... 2484
fide rating. And Kasparov having a rating

00:00:27.109 --> 00:00:33.100
of 2831 at the time. So, let's dive right
in here, again Carlsen on the white end playing

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:40.100
d4. Kasparov responding d5. And we get right
into the Cambridge Springs defense, which

00:00:41.329 --> 00:00:48.329
is denoted by the move queen to a5 which is
doing a few things: first and foremost getting

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:54.920
out of a pin, secondly placing white in a
pin, and you might as well start to already

00:00:54.920 --> 00:01:00.679
view the queen doing a third thing, namely
functioning laterally. Because at any moment

00:01:00.679 --> 00:01:06.720
there can be this capture d takes c and in
many instances it could be netting black some

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:13.560
material. I'll touch on that in a little bit
from now. But before I get to it, let me point

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:20.560
out that if this happens to be an opening
you play as white or black, there is a neat

00:01:22.030 --> 00:01:29.030
little trap, I guess you could say, that could
come about. But it shows itself with a slightly

00:01:29.409 --> 00:01:36.409
tweaked move order, so let me touch on that
very quickly. It runs with after c4, we are

00:01:37.009 --> 00:01:44.009
not having c6 but rather e6. Knight c3, knight
f6, bishop g5, and knight here. So we're looking

00:01:45.909 --> 00:01:52.909
at again a similar position but, the difference
here is that the moves knight f3 and c6 are

00:01:53.490 --> 00:02:00.490
absent. And the thing to be cautious of as
white is to resist the temptation to go ahead

00:02:00.770 --> 00:02:05.969
and win a pawn, because you are not winning
pawn in the following way... this is actually

00:02:05.969 --> 00:02:12.310
falling for what's known as the elephant trap
where after white takes on this square, trying

00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:19.310
to exploit this pin, black can simply ignore
it. And it's a very very temporary queen sac...

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:24.610
black getting the white queen back just a
couple moves later and after the smoke has

00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:30.330
cleared, black is simply up a knight. This
of course is not going to happen in the high-level

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:37.320
games, but something to certainly be aware
of should you already have this as part of

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:44.320
your own opening repertoire... as again white
or black. Getting back on with the actual

00:02:45.310 --> 00:02:51.350
game, after this queen a5 move white takes
immediate action against this pin, breaking

00:02:51.350 --> 00:02:58.350
it with knight d2 and also watching over this
e4 square, either one of these knights is

00:02:58.490 --> 00:03:05.180
now able to watch over that e4 square. And
this is already a very important detail because

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:11.760
if white is just going to be running with
a very natural looking type of move such as

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:18.760
bishop to d3, preparing to castle, watching
over the e4 square. Black could still just

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:24.040
throw that knight into that e4 square, adding
more fuel to that c3 knight, pressure on the

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:31.040
bishop, and there is an interesting sequence
of moves if you know it gets to be too much

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:37.400
on the white end and white wants to remove
one of the attackers of the c3 square, with

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:44.400
bishop takes knight... well now we have d
takes e, which is clearing this 5th rank for

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:51.880
that black queen. So knight e5 is pretty much
the only move to save that bishop. And then

00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:58.880
we kind of get into a little bit of a wacky
variation here after f6 we could have queen

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:07.460
to h5, g6, knight takes, pawn takes, queen
takes rook, queen takes bishop, and this is

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:12.820
a really weird imbalance. But, black is actually
the side that's going to stand better even

00:04:12.820 --> 00:04:18.040
though it seems like their king is a little
bit shaky. The queen alone is not going to

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:23.690
be enough to disrupt that black king's position.
If we are to take it a couple moves further,

00:04:23.690 --> 00:04:29.600
we pretty much have this situation right here
where these bishops are going to be ready

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:35.190
to just strike at that white king very soon.
The bishop maybe coming out to this diagonal,

00:04:35.190 --> 00:04:40.380
the other bishop making use of this diagonal,
and the queen is a difficult piece to be chasing

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:47.380
off of these very weak light squares on the
white side. So again, be aware of that 3rd

00:04:50.850 --> 00:04:57.850
detail when the queen does play to a5 here.
Again, off of the diagonal, functioning on

00:04:57.970 --> 00:05:04.970
this one, and the lateral movement shows itself
in many variations. So, knight d2 breaking

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:12.280
the pin, bishop b4, persistent on this c3
point. Queen to c2, lending support. Black

00:05:13.290 --> 00:05:20.290
castles, bishop e2, and e5. So, striking at
the white center right here with this e5 move

00:05:20.350 --> 00:05:26.820
and at a later point looking to develop this
bishop. However, in this game that bishop

00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:33.820
really does just sit on that c8 square for
some time. In fact, it doesn't even move until

00:05:35.060 --> 00:05:41.480
move 29. So there's quite a bit of stuff that
goes on before this bishop moves, and well

00:05:41.480 --> 00:05:46.620
actually that rook as well. So, the bishop
has to move before this guy can get out of

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:53.240
the corner. So there is an important detail
right here, and it's once again focused on

00:05:53.240 --> 00:06:00.240
this lateral movement. The difference between
e2 and d3 is really the whole game really

00:06:00.639 --> 00:06:06.960
because if you play here, there we go, game
over. Can you believe it? Believe it! Because,

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:12.720
we have the bishop hit and this bishop hit.
And there is no in between move for white

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:19.720
because black says no my in between move is
even stronger taking the bishop, being on

00:06:20.470 --> 00:06:25.800
the queen and only then recapturing. And we
once again have a situation where black is

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:32.800
just up a minor piece. So, the difference
between bishop to d3 and bishop to e2, as

00:06:33.270 --> 00:06:39.520
was played in the game is a very significant
one. So, e5 striking in the center, white

00:06:39.520 --> 00:06:46.520
now fully developed. That's noted by a direct
connection between those rooks. Takes, knight

00:06:46.550 --> 00:06:51.820
b3, hitting the queen. And when I initially
saw this, okay queen goes back to b6, when

00:06:51.820 --> 00:06:56.750
I initially saw this I thought okay the knight
pivoted there hitting the queen and then would

00:06:56.750 --> 00:07:03.750
be recapturing. And I guess this is maybe
a somewhat solid approach to it, not having

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:11.320
to cope with an isolated queen pawn type of
position which is the type of structure we

00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:18.700
end up having to work with on the white end
here. So we'll see how that shows itself.

00:07:20.350 --> 00:07:25.930
What's also important to note is that if in
the situation knight takes pawn was played,

00:07:25.930 --> 00:07:32.550
and the bishop takes the knight, again be
aware of this knight pivot to the e4 square.

00:07:32.550 --> 00:07:38.900
If queen takes, then this is big trouble.
So, white would be needing to capture with

00:07:38.900 --> 00:07:44.550
the pawn in that instance. But, we don't have
the recapture on this square right here with

00:07:44.550 --> 00:07:50.780
that b3 knight, but rather e takes d. And
after d takes c, bishop takes c, there we

00:07:50.780 --> 00:07:57.780
have it isolated queen pawn. And, so this
is a dynamic advantage here for white. White

00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:05.280
is looking to maybe press forward in the center
at some point, change the pawn structure,

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:12.280
and show that this lead in development that
white has is going to prove useful... prove

00:08:13.580 --> 00:08:20.580
to be actually an advantage. So, we have a5
and then a4. This is a very typical idea to

00:08:22.360 --> 00:08:29.360
be aware of... knights that find themselves
on b3 b6 g6 g3 are often chased away with

00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:38.360
these rook pawns. It often becomes very difficult
for that knight to have a good reaction to

00:08:38.919 --> 00:08:44.159
a threat when the rook pawns come down to
attack it. So, white stops that cold with

00:08:44.159 --> 00:08:50.060
a4. And I think that this is a little bit
of an uncomfortable move to have to make,

00:08:50.060 --> 00:08:57.060
I really do like this a5 move. After a4, because
now this is a hole... this bishop is able

00:09:01.330 --> 00:09:06.570
to kind of just sit there, and maybe one day
one of these knights, let's say the one on

00:09:06.570 --> 00:09:12.880
d7 can somehow make use of that square as
well. So, there is a little bit of a concession,

00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:19.020
but the main point is to preserve this knight's
position for the time being. So, queen back

00:09:19.020 --> 00:09:26.020
c7, functioning on this diagonal right now,
that h2 point. Rook to e1, getting it doing

00:09:26.600 --> 00:09:32.810
something, the bishop gets kicked around,
bishop d6, and now h3 and then okay knight

00:09:32.810 --> 00:09:37.750
to b6. Now at this point I was actually, when
I first reviewed it I was looking at g5 and

00:09:37.750 --> 00:09:43.500
then I'm like "well where is the compensation
exactly... where is this compensation?". And

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:46.950
it completely slipped my mind that hey "what
do we have going on here?". This is something

00:09:46.950 --> 00:09:52.650
I should've been thinking all the while, this
pawn is pinned and a pinned piece is a powerless

00:09:52.650 --> 00:09:58.430
one. This could be a deadly check. In fact,
it would just be a winning shot at this very

00:09:58.430 --> 00:10:05.430
early stage. As soon as g5 is played, just
boom! check, take check, knight blocks, grab

00:10:05.930 --> 00:10:11.279
another pawn, the position is crumbling, the
king is dead. So, just be aware of that, that

00:10:11.279 --> 00:10:17.540
bishop on c4. In fact, you might as well already,
in your own internal dialogue, in your own

00:10:17.540 --> 00:10:21.980
thinking when you're playing the game... as
soon as this bishop makes the capture on c4,

00:10:21.980 --> 00:10:26.870
you might as well just start saying "okay,
that pawn is pinned". And maybe there's a

00:10:26.870 --> 00:10:32.470
way to be making use of it, maybe you can
be making use of this square right now. It's

00:10:32.470 --> 00:10:39.200
not super clear where you can be doing that
in this case just yet. But definitely at this

00:10:39.200 --> 00:10:44.800
point right here, as soon as h6 is played
you could be making use of that square one

00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:51.800
day. Anyhow, bishop back, bishop d6, h3, knight
b6 hitting the bishop, and this is definitely

00:10:53.970 --> 00:11:00.970
one of those positions where black has to
come up with something because it's really

00:11:00.990 --> 00:11:06.270
a case where black is in a giant knot. Because
look at these queen side pieces, they are

00:11:06.270 --> 00:11:11.300
just spectators, "what are they doing?". And
what is this knight doing? It's a complete

00:11:11.300 --> 00:11:15.860
domino effect really. If this knight can't
move, this bishop can't move, and if this

00:11:15.860 --> 00:11:22.480
bishop can't move, this rook can't do anything.
And the sad thing is that if this knight does

00:11:22.480 --> 00:11:27.839
move, I mean it does move in the game it goes
to b6, but because of the pressure, this is

00:11:27.839 --> 00:11:32.220
what I'm getting at, because of the pressure
that white has against this knight, it just

00:11:32.220 --> 00:11:39.220
kind of bottles up this whole queen side,
its influence is a tremendous one. So we have

00:11:39.750 --> 00:11:45.850
both sides basically going into a pretty big
think here. Well really Kasparov at this point,

00:11:45.850 --> 00:11:52.850
knight b6. Because we have a couple captures
going on here. There is not just this typical

00:11:53.020 --> 00:11:58.980
capture and then recapture stuff going on.
No, we have after bishop takes on f6, knight

00:11:58.980 --> 00:12:05.980
takes c4, and white doesn't react to the threat
on the bishop, but rather knight to e4, counterattacking

00:12:06.940 --> 00:12:12.029
move, centralizing the knight, hitting the
bishop, and also hitting this knight right

00:12:12.029 --> 00:12:18.810
here. Black responds first inserting a check,
king h1 only move, and now this is where there

00:12:18.810 --> 00:12:25.810
is maybe a slight improvement on the black
end because now it's white who really dictates

00:12:25.980 --> 00:12:32.980
the game and obtains a very strong initiative.
And in fact throughout this whole game white

00:12:33.430 --> 00:12:40.430
was never worse off, white was always slightly
better. And now things become even worse for

00:12:42.370 --> 00:12:48.770
black. In the game we saw knight d6, and the
continuation was king takes bishop which is

00:12:48.770 --> 00:12:55.770
welcoming one of these very devastating moves,
one of these discovered checks. But it's not

00:13:00.020 --> 00:13:05.820
so devastating in this instance right here.
After knight captures on e4 we have just the

00:13:05.820 --> 00:13:12.500
counterattacking bishop to e5, and unfortunately
black is having to work themselves back into

00:13:12.500 --> 00:13:19.500
a pin in order to preserve that knight. The
alternative is to not play knight d6 but rather

00:13:20.100 --> 00:13:27.100
b5, maintaining this knight's position and
I believe white would soon be having to react

00:13:27.250 --> 00:13:33.750
to just this pawn takes bishop move. But we
don't have that, b5 was not played. The knight

00:13:33.750 --> 00:13:40.750
actually reacts to that queen threat, knight
d6, Carlsen working himself into a discovered

00:13:45.970 --> 00:13:51.750
attack. Knight takes, bishop back, knight
d6 and here we go. If one of the pieces is

00:13:51.750 --> 00:13:57.740
in a pin, what do you do? You add fuel to
it, queen c5, two pieces hitting, and again

00:13:57.740 --> 00:14:03.240
white is dictating the game from here... has
the initiative in other words. d5, throwing

00:14:03.240 --> 00:14:09.500
punches at black, hitting at c6, vacating
d4. The knight is looking to centralize itself,

00:14:09.500 --> 00:14:15.580
all the while these two again are still just
camping out here on the queen side, not doing

00:14:15.580 --> 00:14:22.580
thing. So queen d7, a natural reaction to
being in a pin, get out of it. Knight d4,

00:14:23.500 --> 00:14:30.500
more pressure on c6, and knight to f5. An
important point is this one right here. In

00:14:31.480 --> 00:14:38.480
fact if you want to stop the video and see
what would you do in this instance right here.

00:14:39.250 --> 00:14:43.640
Again if you'd like to pause the video, what
would be the winning continuation for white

00:14:43.640 --> 00:14:50.540
in this position? Okay, the sequence of moves
which would absolutely kill black is just

00:14:50.540 --> 00:14:56.930
to take the knight and then at the end of
it we're having this check and then again,

00:14:56.930 --> 00:15:01.170
not the first time we're seeing this idea,
piling up on the pinned piece, the pinned

00:15:01.170 --> 00:15:08.170
piece is the bishop, it's dead, and so too
will be that black king soon enough. So in

00:15:08.940 --> 00:15:15.940
other words, c takes d, don't do it. Knight
f5 instead, d takes c, on the queen, b takes

00:15:17.910 --> 00:15:22.220
c, knight takes pawn, white is up a pawn.
The knight is within checking distance, hitting

00:15:22.220 --> 00:15:27.959
the rook. Things are very uncomfortable for
black. Again these guys, a couple clowns right

00:15:27.959 --> 00:15:34.959
here on the queen side. Rook to e8 in the
meantime, rook d1, queen e6, and we have rook

00:15:35.730 --> 00:15:42.670
f to e1 which is a very natural looking move.
It's very human-like to be making this type

00:15:42.670 --> 00:15:49.670
of move, it's just tidying everything up,
centralizing these rooks, and now we just

00:15:50.209 --> 00:15:57.209
have all of the white pieces functioning.
However the improvement is going to be this

00:15:57.490 --> 00:16:04.490
move right here which is well I guess not
so human-like because it's not harmonious.

00:16:06.240 --> 00:16:13.240
This bishop right now on that c7 square is
not protected. When there's this rook f to

00:16:13.649 --> 00:16:18.950
e1 move, again everything is nice and cozy
here, everything is defended, the rooks guarding

00:16:18.950 --> 00:16:24.010
one another, controlling all these central
squares. But this bishop to c7 move would

00:16:24.010 --> 00:16:31.010
have been the more accurate thing to do. I'm
not aware of what the time constraints were,

00:16:31.709 --> 00:16:37.690
or what the time controls were at that very
moment. But what is this move doing? It's

00:16:37.690 --> 00:16:43.630
just adding what is now a 3rd attacker to
that a5 pawn, and if that goes we're going

00:16:43.630 --> 00:16:50.149
to have these pawns right here, these connected
passed a and b pawns, it's going to be a very

00:16:50.149 --> 00:16:57.149
strong thing. So, it was not played and instead
again rook f to e1. And finally, move 29,

00:16:57.329 --> 00:17:04.329
we are having that queen bishop do something.
So it throws itself on a very good diagonal,

00:17:05.709 --> 00:17:10.529
main light square diagonal. And now we have
the knight coming back, this fork going on

00:17:10.529 --> 00:17:17.529
and actually even though white is up a pawn,
there is not really going to be a way to convert

00:17:18.359 --> 00:17:25.359
it. In fact this endgame just kind of goes
to nothing right here. It gets to a position

00:17:28.059 --> 00:17:34.690
where white really can't make use of that
extra pawn because it's in large part to these

00:17:34.690 --> 00:17:41.159
guys being on opposite colors. Opposite colored
bishops will often translate into a draw,

00:17:41.159 --> 00:17:47.350
and this position right here is no exception.
So we just have a lot of little logical moves

00:17:47.350 --> 00:17:53.749
going on here, the meeting of one move threats.
Attack of the pawn, defense of the pawn, that

00:17:53.749 --> 00:18:00.649
sort of stuff. Additionally when this bishop
was threatened right here with f6, what do

00:18:00.649 --> 00:18:04.419
you want to do? You want to react but still
be aggressive in some way. So that's why it's

00:18:04.419 --> 00:18:09.320
coming to that c3 square. So the rooks come
off, the rook defends, the kings improve their

00:18:09.320 --> 00:18:14.419
position. This is an endgame, the kings need
to be doing stuff. And now the bishop is looking

00:18:14.419 --> 00:18:21.419
to make sure this pawn doesn't get out of
control. a5, bishop back, f3, and you'll notice

00:18:21.840 --> 00:18:28.509
that it's not by chance that we're kind of
having right here on both sides these sorts

00:18:28.509 --> 00:18:35.019
of structures. You really don't want to be
too careless right here with let's say white

00:18:35.019 --> 00:18:41.440
making a move like f4. I'm sure it's still
