00:18:46.470 --> 00:18:53.470
2nd rank and easily converge on that g2 square.
However, if this type of structure is maintained,

00:18:53.970 --> 00:19:00.970
the pawns on f3 and g2, and similarly g7 and
f6, both structures are combating, or taking

00:19:02.739 --> 00:19:08.679
into account the opposing bishops and how
they can maybe be putting pressure on the

00:19:08.679 --> 00:19:15.679
base points g7 and g2. So just a quick point
to be making. This does turn out to just be

00:19:17.169 --> 00:19:23.340
a draw. We have rook to h1, getting out of
this pin. If there is any chance for a win

00:19:23.340 --> 00:19:29.109
on the white end, they are needing to keep
the rooks on the board. And so that's why

00:19:29.109 --> 00:19:34.269
we are having white throw in a check, the
king goes back, the bishop plays to c5. The

00:19:34.269 --> 00:19:40.779
rook now has the opportunity to get to this
active 2nd rank and get behind the passed

00:19:40.779 --> 00:19:46.009
pawn. This is the ideal placement for a rook
defending against a passed pawn, you would

00:19:46.009 --> 00:19:53.009
like to get it behind the pawn. King goes
back to g3, defense. Rook a2, bishop b6, king

00:19:53.809 --> 00:20:00.509
f7, rook c1, getting this pawn involved guarding
some dark squares against that white king.

00:20:00.509 --> 00:20:06.859
Rook check, king g6, rook c6, placing the
king in a pin or that pawn in a pin, maybe

00:20:06.859 --> 00:20:13.859
looking bishop to d4. But now black can put
pressure on this pawn right here, and white

00:20:13.869 --> 00:20:20.869
just defends by just playing bishop to f2,
and a draw was agreed at this point. So 13-year-old

00:20:20.989 --> 00:20:27.989
Magnus Carlsen drawing the legendary Garry
Kasparov. Really really incredible thing to

00:20:30.279 --> 00:20:36.679
